{
  "segments": [
    {
      "id": 20,
      "text": "Fell into the exact same structural trap. Which was: they were completely centralized."
    },
    {
      "id": 21,
      "text": "Ah. Meaning they still had like a central server or a corporate headquarters managing the final settlement of the money."
    },
    {
      "id": 22,
      "text": "Right. And centralized architecture creates a massive single point of failure. Just a giant target."
    },
    {
      "id": 23,
      "text": "Exactly. If you build a centralized digital currency, you are essentially painting a giant bullseye on a single server."
    },
    {
      "id": 24,
      "text": "Hackers targeted them relentlessly. I can imagine. And governments, who were obviously nervous about unregulated money, they"
    },
    {
      "id": 25,
      "text": "just litigated them out of existence. Wow. Or, you know, in some cases, the parent company simply ran out of funding and liquidated,"
    },
    {
      "id": 26,
      "text": "leaving the users holding what? Just worthless digital tokens. Completely worthless. Yikes. But the engineers didn't just"
    },
    {
      "id": 27,
      "text": "build centralized servers because they wanted to be in charge, right? No, no, they had to. Because of the double-spend problem. Exactly. The double"
    },
    {
      "id": 28,
      "text": "spend problem. Right. Because, OK, think about it like this: if I have an MP3 file on my computer, I can copy it and send it to 10 different"
    },
    {
      "id": 29,
      "text": "friends, and everyone gets a perfect copy. Right. But if I have a digital dollar, what's stopping me from right-clicking, hitting copy,"
    },
    {
      "id": 30,
      "text": "and spending it 10 times at 10 different stores? Which would destroy the currency instantly. Without a central bank checking"
    },
    {
      "id": 31,
      "text": "my account balance and updating their master spreadsheet every single time I swipe my card, how do you prevent me from spending the same"
    },
    {
      "id": 32,
      "text": "digital dollar twice? And that is the core dilemma that blocked digital currency for 20 years. 20 years of being stuck on"
    },
    {
      "id": 33,
      "text": "that one problem. Yeah. How do you create a system where no one is in charge, there is no central server, yet every"
    },
    {
      "id": 34,
      "text": "single participant agrees exactly on who owns what? Enter the mysterious Satoshi Nakamoto."
    },
    {
      "id": 35,
      "text": "Because in 2008, this person or group of people—I mean, we still"
    },
    {
      "id": 36,
      "text": "still have absolutely no idea who they are—published a white paper proposing a peer-to-peer electronic cash system. And the"
    },
    {
      "id": 37,
      "text": "network actually launched in 2009. Right. And then by 2011, Satoshi just vanished. Just walked away. Yeah, completely"
    },
    {
      "id": 38,
      "text": "gone, leaving behind this open-source, totally decentralized system. But to make this work, Satoshi had to solve a legendary"
    },
    {
      "id": 39,
      "text": "computer science dilemma, right? The Byzantine Generals Problem. Yes. The Byzantine Generals Problem. Yeah. It is a fascinating"
    }
  ]
}
